<h2>Which Policy Responds to Storm Damage?</h2>
<p>On the Coastal Bend, the answer depends on the cause of each part of the damage and on how your coverage is set up:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Wind and hail</strong> are covered by your homeowners or commercial policy unless it excludes them. In Nueces, San Patricio, Aransas, and Kleberg counties, many policies do, and the wind and hail coverage comes from a TWIA windstorm policy instead.</li>
<li><strong>Rain that enters</strong> through a storm-created opening is often covered under the policy that covers the wind or hail that made the opening.</li>
<li><strong>Flood and storm surge</strong> are generally excluded from homeowners and commercial policies and are covered only by an NFIP or private flood policy.</li>
<li><strong>Lightning and fallen trees</strong> are typically covered by the main property policy, subject to its terms and limits.</li>
</ul>
<p>When a storm produces both wind and flood damage, each insurer may argue the other is responsible. Clear documentation of what happened, and in what order, matters.</p>

[[cta-storm]]

<h2>Documenting Storm Damage</h2>
<p>The goal is to show what was damaged, when, and by what. A strong storm claim file usually includes:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Photos and video</strong> of every elevation, the roof, and each damaged room, taken before cleanup and repeated after tarping.</li>
<li><strong>Weather evidence</strong> such as NOAA Storm Events entries, NWS storm surveys, and nearby station data. See our guide to <a href="/noaa-weather-records-document-property-damage-corpus-christi/">using NOAA weather records</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Water lines</strong> measured and photographed if flooding was involved.</li>
<li><strong>A contents inventory</strong> listing damaged items with descriptions, ages, and replacement prices.</li>
<li><strong>Receipts</strong> for emergency repairs, water extraction, tree removal, and temporary housing.</li>
<li><strong>A line-item estimate</strong> for the full repair scope, including code items.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> lays out each item.</p>

<h2>Claim Timing and Texas Prompt Payment Rules</h2>
<p>For many insurers, Texas Insurance Code Chapter 542 sets deadlines once a claim is reported: acknowledge the claim and begin the investigation within 15 days, accept or reject within 15 business days after receiving the items requested, and pay within 5 business days after accepting. After a weather-related catastrophe or major natural disaster, as defined by the Commissioner, these deadlines are extended by 15 days (<a href="https://tcss.legis.texas.gov/resources/IN/htm/IN.542.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Tex. Ins. Code Ch. 542</a>). TWIA claims follow separate deadlines under Chapter 2210; see <a href="/services/twia-windstorm-claims/">TWIA windstorm claims</a>. Large storms can still slow things down, and our <a href="/services/delayed-insurance-claims/">delayed insurance claims</a> page covers what to do when a claim stalls.</p>

<h2>Common Problems on Storm Claims</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Cause disputes</strong> &ndash; wear and tear, prior damage, or flood blamed instead of wind or hail.</li>
<li><strong>Incomplete scope</strong> &ndash; estimates that miss interior damage, other structures, or code-required items.</li>
<li><strong>Deductible surprises</strong> &ndash; percentage wind/hail or named-storm deductibles that are larger than expected.</li>
<li><strong>Depreciation</strong> &ndash; heavy depreciation on older roofs and contents.</li>
<li><strong>Missed deadlines</strong> &ndash; late notice or missed TWIA or policy deadlines.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Storm Claims for Businesses and Rental Properties</h2>
<p>Commercial storm claims add layers that homeowners rarely face. Low-slope roofs on retail centers, warehouses, and offices can hold water after a puncture, soaking insulation across a wide area before a leak shows up inside. Signs, rooftop HVAC units, canopies, and parking lot lighting are often damaged in the same storm. If the business has to close or slow down, business income and extra expense coverage may apply, but those claims usually require detailed financial records showing what the interruption cost. Owners of rental homes and apartment buildings may also have loss of rents coverage, subject to policy terms. Starting documentation on the day of the storm, and keeping it organized, can make a large difference later. Our <a href="/services/commercial-property-claims/">commercial property claims</a> page goes into more detail, including how commercial losses are usually documented.</p>

<h2>How a Corpus Christi Public Adjuster May Help</h2>
<p>A Corpus Christi Public Adjuster represents the policyholder only. After a storm, that may include reviewing each of your policies, inspecting the property, building a detailed estimate, gathering weather evidence, separating wind damage from water damage as far as the evidence allows, and handling communication with each insurer. We serve Corpus Christi, Nueces County, and surrounding Coastal Bend communities, from Portland and Ingleside to Rockport, Port Aransas, and Kingsville.</p>
<p>We cannot promise any outcome; every claim depends on the policy and the facts. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>

<h2>Your Responsibilities After a Storm</h2>
<ol>
<li>Give prompt notice to every insurer that may be involved.</li>
<li>Take reasonable steps to prevent more damage and keep the receipts.</li>
<li>Preserve damaged materials or photograph them before disposal.</li>
<li>Answer information requests and submit any proof of loss on time.</li>
<li>Keep a claim log of calls, emails, inspections, and payments.</li>
</ol>
<p>If you have questions about a lawsuit, bad faith, or the deadline to sue, consult a Texas attorney.</p>
